window.docContents[127].resolve({"/Judgments-and-domains/Domains/#Core-C______--Judgments-and-domains--Domains":{"contents":"A location  is a natural number. The store keeps a counter of the next free location. Allocation takes the counter and increments it, and the removal of a location never decreases it, so locations are never reused.\n\nThe values are , , , the pointer , the value  of nullptr, the object , a record of one location per field with its class tag, and the instance  of an object type  of the library, such as a vector, with one location per element, .The value  is the result of a call to a function without return value. The closure is a value too, .The tag of an object is the class that new names when it creates the object. Its fields are those of the whole chain of classes, the root base first. The tag decides the dispatch of virtual methods and the destructors that delete runs. Objects and instances of object types of the library live in the store at their own location, and a program reaches them only through pointers and references.The default value of a type, which new gives to every field and to every element of a vector, is ,  or .\n\nThe type int has 32 bits in two's complement. Every rule that gives an integer has the premise , the test Int32.inRange, so a result outside the range has no derivation. The function  of the evaluator returns the integer when it lies in the range and error otherwise.C++17 fixes neither the width nor the representation of int. A plain int has the natural size suggested by the architecture of the execution environment, with a range of at least  (N4659 §6.9.1 paragraph 2 and §21.3.5, N1570 §5.2.4.2.1). Its representation may be two's complement, ones' complement or signed magnitude (N4659 §6.9.1 paragraph 7). The range of int is therefore a property of the platform.The ABI of the platform fixes it. The System V AMD64 psABI and AAPCS64 both give int 32 bits, and GCC supports only two's complement integer types.An arithmetic result outside the range of int has undefined behaviour (N4659 §8 paragraph 4), and Core C++ makes it error. That boundary has to coincide with the one of g++ and clang on x86‑64 and ARM64, the implementations Core C++ follows. A wider range would give a value where C++ gives none, and a narrower one would give error where C++ gives a value.The representation enters only through the lower bound . Core C++ has no bitwise operators, no shifts and no unsigned types, so no program observes a bit pattern. The width lives in Int32.min and Int32.max, and in the largest integer literal the lexer of  accepts.\n\nThe environment  is a finite map from identifiers to locations, and the most recent binding prevails.Each binding records whether the declaration that made it allocated the location, as τ x = e does, or aliased an existing one, as the reference τ& y = e does. The notation  extends  with an owned binding, Env.extend, and  with an alias binding, Env.alias. The set , Env.fresh, holds the locations of the owned bindings of  that  does not have. Scope exit frees these locations at the end of a block, of a for loop and of a call, so an alias never frees the location it names.Inside a member body, this is an alias binding to the location of the receiver, made by the call and never freed by the return.\n\nThe store  is a finite map from locations to values, with a counter of the next location. The operation  returns the location of the counter, , and the store  with the counter advanced, and allocMany allocates one location per value, in order. The operation  removes the locations of  from the domain and leaves the counter, so a freed location is never allocated again. A rule that reads or writes a location has the premise , so an access outside the domain has no derivation, and the evaluator gives error.\n\nThe value of a lambda is the closure , with the parameters, the result type, the body and the captured copies, one value per variable of the body, other than a parameter, that the environment binds.A std::function object of  holds a closure in its one location, as the target of the object, or  for the function with no target. No variable, field or element holds a closure directly, since a std::function is an object, reached by pointer or by reference. The type of a lambda is the function type , which Ty.isFn recognises, and it occurs only as the template argument of std::function.\n\nThe control result  of a command is  or . A  interrupts sequence, block and loop up to the call that consumes it. A call whose body ends with  gives  when the result type is void, and error otherwise.\n\nThe result error is not a value of the language. In the relations it is the absence of a derivation. Whatever C++17 leaves undefined fails a premise of every rule that could reach it, , , , , and the subject has no derivation, with nothing to propagate. The evaluator, which runs the relations, reports the case as error, with a message and the exit code 134, and the error replaces the result of the whole program.Its causes are division by zero, int overflow, the dereference of nullptr, an index outside a vector, a negative vector size, a failed assert, the call of a std::function with no target, a location outside , a missing return in a non void function, a second delete of the same object, a delete through a pointer to a base class without a virtual destructor, and the exhaustion of the fuel of the evaluator, a bound on the depth of a derivation that a program without termination reaches. The causes undeclaredVariable, undeclaredFunction, arity, typeError and notCallable guard the evaluator against a program that skipped the type checker.\n\nThe typing context  is a finite map from identifiers to types. The types are , , , a class , a pointer , an instance  of a class template of the library, a function type  as template argument, and the internal type  of nullptr.Class types and the types of the library whose module gives new a signature, std::vector and std::function, are object types, IsObject. They have no values, and no variable, by value parameter, result or field has one, Storable. A reference parameter and a local reference may have an object type, Bindable. An expression of object type, such as *p, never stands where a value is expected, HasValues. It occurs as the left operand of ., as the operand of [] or of an operator member, as the callee of (*f)(ē), as the argument of a reference parameter and as the initialiser of a local reference.The judgment  is the signature that the module of  gives to the creation of an instance of , , the relation StaticModule.new. The flag  of  is true for a reference.Each binding carries a mark, read only or not. The check of a lambda body sets the mark on every binding of the enclosing scope, so that the body reads the copies of [=] and never writes them. The binding of this gives the current class, TEnv.self, inside a member body.\n\nThe judgment  states that the type  is well formed. The program declares every class it names, . A header declares every type of the library it names, with its number of template arguments, , Program.libDecls, and the module of  accepts the arguments, . A function type has a storable or void result and storable parameters. The judgment does not depend on .The relation  states that a value of type  is accepted where  is expected. It holds of equal types, of nullptr and a pointer type in either order, and of a pointer to a class and a pointer to one of its bases. The relation , Program.subclass, holds when  or  derives from . These are the only implicit conversions of Core C++.The relation is directional. Its left side is the type of an expression and its right side the type its context requires. The judgment  of  uses it for an initialiser, the right side of an assignment, an argument and a returned value. The rule T-Eq tries it in both directions, and the join of the conditional chooses its type with it, both in .The rule C-Null is the null pointer conversion of C++ (N4659 §7.11 paragraph 1), so Node* p = nullptr;, p = nullptr; and return nullptr; type check. No declared type is , so every nullptr ends up converted to a pointer type. The rule C-Sub lets a pointer to a derived class stand where a pointer to its base is expected, and never the converse, since Core C++ has no cast.Compatibility and subsumption are two relations. Subsumption  is a relation on classes, the subclass order of the program, reflexive and transitive. It extends to types,  when  or when both are classes in that order, Typing.refCompat. It says that an object of class  is also an object of class . It therefore applies where the semantics binds or chooses a location, the initialiser of a reference in  and the branches of a conditional that denotes a location in . No value moves there, and the object keeps its class. Compatibility  is a relation on the types of values. It says that a value of type  may stand where the context expects a value of type . It contains subsumption only through pointers, by C-Sub, since a class type has no values, and it adds the conversions of nullptr that subsumption knows nothing of.The relation  is not an equivalence. It is not symmetric, since  holds for a proper base  and  does not. It is not transitive either, since  and  hold for two unrelated classes  and  while  does not. The rule C-NullR causes this, and Core C++ keeps it. Without C-NullR the relation would be a preorder.\n\nThe function environment is the program  seen as a finite map from a name to the overload set of the functions of that name, funsNamed, and to the declarations of the library, libDecls. The program is a parameter of every relation, which reads it and never changes it. The evaluator selects a function by the signature the type checker wrote into the call, lookupSig, and the static relation by pickFun. The syntax of the declarations it holds is in .\n\nThe classes of the program form the class table, a finite map from class names to declarations. A declaration has an optional base, fields and methods with their visibility, at most one constructor and at most one destructor.The table gives, for a class, its chain up to the root base, every field of the chain with the class that declares it, the nearest method of a given name, the overload set of a method name, one method per signature, the subclass relation and whether some class of the chain has a virtual destructor.The type checker consults it for types, visibility and dispatch, and new and delete for the fields to allocate and to free. A name carries the qualification of its namespace, N::C.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Judgments and domains","header":"2.1. Domains","id":"/Judgments-and-domains/Domains/#Core-C______--Judgments-and-domains--Domains"},"/Lexer-and-grammar/Lexer/#Core-C______--Lexer-and-grammar--Lexer":{"contents":"Tokens fall in six classes, and the end token eof closes the array.* Reserved words, the 24 words int, bool, void, if, else, while, for, return, true, false, auto, delete, new, nullptr, this, class, public, private, virtual, override, namespace, template, typename and operator.* Type identifiers, TypeId, with an uppercase initial.* Variable identifiers, VarId, with a lowercase initial or _, naming variables, fields, functions and methods.* Namespace identifiers, NsId, the identifiers of either case that :: follows.* Decimal integer literals, IntLit.* Operators and punctuation, with [=] as a single token by the longest match rule.There is no token >>, so Stack<Stack<int>> closes with two tokens >.The lexer covers the input by a sequence of Token and Skip, discards Skip, and takes the longest match at each position. A line comment ends at a newline or at the end of the input. The automaton tries the symbols of three characters before those of two and of one.\n\nThe function identifier first looks an identifier up in the reserved words and then classifies it by its initial. A further pass over the token array, qualifiers, marks as an NsId every identifier whose next token is ::, of either case.The pass runs after the automaton, so it sees neither white space nor comments and marks std :: vector as it marks std::vector. The function parseUnit of  runs the automaton on each line of the Preproc output alone and the pass once on the tokens of all the lines, so a :: that starts a line marks the identifier that ends the line before.That class is what lets a qualified name open a type where the case convention alone would read a variable, so std::vector<int> needs no reserved word.\n\nThe function lex maps a string to an array of tokens ended by eof. It runs the automaton Lexer.run and then the pass of . It discards white space and line comments, reads the longest run of digits as an integer literal, the longest run of identifier characters as an identifier, and tries the symbols of three, two and one characters in that order. Any other character is a lexical error.The lexer also rejects what C++ would read otherwise, so that every Core C++ program is a C++ program with the same meaning.* It rejects a keyword of C++ or an alternative representation such as and outside the reserved words of the subset (N4659 §5.11, Tables 5 and 6).* It rejects an integer literal of two or more digits with a leading 0, which C++ reads in octal, and one above 2147483647, to which C++ gives a type wider than int.* It reads -- and ++ as tokens that no production uses, so 5--2 fails as in C++, where the longest match gives --.The automaton Lexer.run is partial, so Lean records an opaque constant that carries the type and not the body, and no property of it is proved here.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Lexer and grammar","header":"1.1. Lexer","id":"/Lexer-and-grammar/Lexer/#Core-C______--Lexer-and-grammar--Lexer"},"/Types-and-expressions/Rules-of-the-chapter/#Core-C______--Types-and-expressions--Rules-of-the-chapter":{"contents":"Every typing rule and every evaluation rule of this chapter, in the order of its sections. The section named above each rule states it with its explanation and with the definition of its notation. The chapters on encapsulation, on type systems and on the library state the rules of the same expressions that involve classes. These are T-VarField, T-LocVarField and LocVarField for a field of this, T-OpBin for an operator member, T-New with a constructor and T-NewLib.\n\nLiterals\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\nVariable\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\nUnary operators\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\nBinary operators\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nConditional\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nObjects and pointers\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Types and expressions","header":"3.7. Rules of the chapter","id":"/Types-and-expressions/Rules-of-the-chapter/#Core-C______--Types-and-expressions--Rules-of-the-chapter"}});